1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,450
Section 4

2
00:00:01,450 --> 00:00:06,890
In public discussion of business, we take certain values for granted.

3
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:09,843
Today I'm going to talk about four of them:

4
00:00:09,843 --> 00:00:16,310
collaboration, hard work, creativity and excellence.

5
00:00:16,310 --> 00:00:20,110
Most people would say they're all 'good things'.

6
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:23,970
I'm going to suggest that's an over-simple view.

7
00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:30,739
The trouble with these values is that they're theoretical concepts, removed from the reality

8
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:32,190
of day-to-day business.

9
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:34,330
Pursue values by all means,

10
00:00:34,330 --> 00:00:38,610
but be prepared for what may happen as a result.

11
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:40,672
They can actually cause damage,

12
00:00:40,672 --> 00:00:43,560
which is not at all the intention.

13
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,750
Business leaders generally try to do the right thing.

14
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:54,112
But all too often the right thing backfires, if those leaders adopt values without understanding

15
00:00:54,112 --> 00:00:57,081
and managing the side effects that arise.

16
00:00:57,081 --> 00:01:02,170
The values can easily get in the way of what's actually intended.

17
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:02,744
OK.

18
00:01:02,744 --> 00:01:08,480
So the first value I'm going to discuss is collaboration.

19
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,208
Er, let me give you an example.

20
00:01:11,208 --> 00:01:16,274
On a management training course I once attended, we were put into groups

21
00:01:16,274 --> 00:01:21,731
and had to construct a bridge across a stream, using building blocks that we

22
00:01:21,731 --> 00:01:22,510
were given.

23
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:28,154
The rule was that everyone in the team had to move at least one

24
00:01:28,154 --> 00:01:30,170
building block during the construction.

25
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:32,923
This was intended to encourage teamwork.

26
00:01:32,923 --> 00:01:37,970
But it was really a job best done by one person.

27
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:42,075
The other teams tried to collaborate on building the structure,

28
00:01:42,075 --> 00:01:46,590
and descended into confusion, with everyone getting in each other's way.

29
00:01:46,590 --> 00:01:50,410
Our team leader solved the challenge brilliantly.

30
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:55,094
She simply asked everyone in the team to move a piece a few centimetres,

31
00:01:55,094 --> 00:01:56,767
to comply with the rule,

32
00:01:56,767 --> 00:02:01,452
and then let the person in the team with an aptitude for puzzles like

33
00:02:01,452 --> 00:02:02,790
this build it alone.

34
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:05,703
We finished before any other team.

35
00:02:05,703 --> 00:02:11,043
My point is that the task wasn't really suited to teamworking,

36
00:02:11,043 --> 00:02:13,470
so why make it one?

37
00:02:13,470 --> 00:02:20,320
Teamwork can also lead to inconsistency - a common cause of poor sales.

38
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:26,381
In the case of a smartphone that a certain company launched, one director wanted

39
00:02:26,381 --> 00:02:28,546
to target the business market,

40
00:02:28,546 --> 00:02:32,010
and another demanded it was aimed at consumers.

41
00:02:32,010 --> 00:02:36,050
The company wanted both directors to be involved,

42
00:02:36,050 --> 00:02:39,585
so gave the product a consumer-friendly name,

43
00:02:39,585 --> 00:02:42,110
but marketed it to companies.

44
00:02:42,110 --> 00:02:46,300
The result was that it met the needs of neither group.

45
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:53,975
It would have been better to let one director or the other have his

46
00:02:53,975 --> 00:02:56,715
way, not both.Now industriousness,

47
00:02:56,715 --> 00:02:58,360
or hard work.

48
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,556
It's easy to mock people who say they work hard:

49
00:03:02,556 --> 00:03:07,591
after all, a hamster running around in a wheel is working hard -

50
00:03:07,591 --> 00:03:08,850
and getting nowhere.

51
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:11,823
Of course hard work is valuable,

52
00:03:11,823 --> 00:03:14,300
but only when properly targeted.

53
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:20,053
Otherwise it wastes the resources that companies value most - time and energy.

54
00:03:20,053 --> 00:03:22,930
And that's bad for the organisation.

55
00:03:22,930 --> 00:03:29,874
There's a management model that groups people according to four criteria:

56
00:03:29,874 --> 00:03:33,030
clever, hard-working, stupid and lazy.

57
00:03:33,030 --> 00:03:39,375
Here 'lazy' means having a rational determination not to carry out unnecessary tasks.

58
00:03:39,375 --> 00:03:43,280
It doesn't mean trying to avoid work altogether.

59
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:46,764
Most people display two of these characteristics,

60
00:03:46,764 --> 00:03:53,235
and the most valuable people are those who are both clever and lazy:

61
00:03:53,235 --> 00:03:55,226
they possess intellectual clarity,

62
00:03:55,226 --> 00:03:58,710
and they don't rush into making decisions.

63
00:03:58,710 --> 00:04:04,560
They come up with solutions to save the time and energy spent by the stupid

64
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:05,730
and hard-working group.

65
00:04:05,730 --> 00:04:09,925
Instead of throwing more man-hours at a problem, the clever

66
00:04:09,925 --> 00:04:13,700
and lazy group looks for a more effective solution.

67
00:04:13,700 --> 00:04:16,748
Next we come to creativity.

68
00:04:16,748 --> 00:04:24,671
This often works well - creating an attention-grabbing TV commercial, for example, might lead

69
00:04:24,671 --> 00:04:26,500
to increased sales.

70
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:30,053
But it isn't always a good thing.

71
00:04:30,053 --> 00:04:37,160
Some advertising campaigns are remembered for their creativity, without having any effect on sales.

72
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:42,234
This happened a few years ago with the launch of a chocolate bar:

73
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:46,137
subsequent research showed that plenty of consumers remembered the adverts,

74
00:04:46,137 --> 00:04:49,260
but had no idea what was being advertised.

75
00:04:49,260 --> 00:04:54,720
The trouble is that the creator derives pleasure from coming up with the idea,

76
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:59,400
and wrongly assumes the audience for the campaign will share that feeling.

77
00:04:59,400 --> 00:05:05,275
A company that brings out thousands of new products may seem more creative than

78
00:05:05,275 --> 00:05:08,213
a company that only has a few,

79
00:05:08,213 --> 00:05:10,731
but it may be too creative,

80
00:05:10,731 --> 00:05:12,410
and make smaller profits.

81
00:05:12,410 --> 00:05:19,120
Creativity needs to be targeted, to solve a problem that the company has identified.

82
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:26,425
Just coming up with more and more novel products isn't necessarily a good thing.

83
00:05:26,425 --> 00:05:27,990
And finally, excellence.

84
00:05:27,990 --> 00:05:32,301
We all know companies that claim they 'strive for excellence',

85
00:05:32,301 --> 00:05:36,180
but it takes a long time to achieve excellence.

86
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:42,676
In business, being first with a product is more profitable than having the best

87
00:05:42,676 --> 00:05:43,140
product.

88
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:49,569
A major study of company performance compared pioneers - that is, companies bringing out

89
00:05:49,569 --> 00:05:55,997
the first version of a particular product – with followers, the companies that copied

90
00:05:55,997 --> 00:05:58,470
and improved on that product.

91
00:05:58,470 --> 00:06:05,097
The study found that the pioneers commanded an average market share of 29 percent,

92
00:06:05,097 --> 00:06:11,250
while the followers achieved less than half that, only 13 percent - even though

93
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:14,090
their product might have been better.

94
00:06:14,090 --> 00:06:20,256
Insisting on excellence in everything we do is time-consuming, wastes energy

95
00:06:20,256 --> 00:06:24,180
and leads to losing out on opportunities.

96
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:29,700
Sometimes, second-rate work is more worthwhile than excellence.

97
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:35,184
'Make sure it's excellent' sounds like a good approach to business,

98
00:06:35,184 --> 00:06:40,170
but the 'just-get-started' approach is likely to be more successful.
